1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
In the 9th century, somewhere in the mountains of Sichuan or Shani province in China, a group of Tauist alchemists were trying to do something that had absolutely nothing to do with warfare.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
They were trying to live forever.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Convinced that the right combination of minerals could produce an elixir of immortality, they spent years mixing, heating, and experimenting with whatever

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:29,000
substances they could get their hands on, following recipes passed down through generations of mystical and medicinal tradition. At some point, one particular batch combined three

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:38,000
ingredients: salt peter, charcoal, and sulfur. And instead of granting eternal life, that mixture did something considerably more dramatic. It exploded.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:47,000
There are accounts of singed beards and burned down workshops scattered through the early historical record, which tells you everything you need to know about how that first encounter actually went.

7
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Nobody in that room was trying to invent a weapon. They called the resulting substance huo yao, meaning fire medicine. a name that still carries the

8
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:03,000
faint, slightly embarrassing memory of its original purpose as a failed pharmaceutical rather than a future engine of conquest. And yet that one

9
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:12,000
accidental beard singing batch of failed immortality medicine eventually became the single most consequential chemical discovery in the history of human

10
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:21,000
conflict. The seed from which every gun, every cannon, and eventually every firearm on Earth would grow. So how did humans get from a failed potion in a

11
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:31,000
towist alchemy lab to the firearm? The answer takes us through bamboo tubes strapped to spears. A siege that proved the new technology actually worked.

12
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Several centuries of careful refinement and a slow, steady migration of knowledge across an entire continent that eventually reshaped the balance of

13
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:48,000
power on every inhabited land mass on Earth. Let's start with what that early gunpowder mixture actually was in the simplest possible terms. Because

14
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:56,000
understanding the basic chemistry helps explain why everything that followed happened the way it did. The mixture those alchemists stumbled onto combined

15
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:05,000
potassium nitrate known as salt peter with charcoal and sulfur in roughly a 75 to 15 to 10 ratio by weight. The salt

16
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:14,000
peter functioned as an oxidizer meaning it released oxygen rapidly when heated, feeding an extremely fast combustion reaction that produced a sudden violent

17
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:21,000
expansion of gas. That expanding gas is the entire secret behind everything gunpowder would eventually be used for.

18
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Whether that meant launching fireworks into the night sky or eventually launching a metal projectile out of a tube with enough force to punch through

19
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:37,000
armor. In its earliest documented uses, gunpowder wasn't weaponized at all. It was used for fireworks, smoke signals, and festival entertainment. Exactly the

20
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:46,000
kind of celebratory decorative application you'd expect from something originally discovered by monks chasing immortality rather than military engineers chasing battlefield advantage.

21
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:54,000
For generations, ordinary people across China encountered this substance primarily as spectacle rather than threat, lighting up festivals and

22
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:02,000
religious ceremonies with bursts of color and noise that had nothing to do with combat at all. That changed once military commanders during the Song Dynasty got a look at what this volatile

23
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:10,000
new substance could actually do. Between the 10th and 12th centuries, Chinese engineers developed something called the Huo Chiang or fire lance. And the design

24
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:18,000
was genuinely about as simple as a weapon could possibly be. Take a length of bamboo, strap it firmly to the end of a spear, pack the tube with gunpowder,

25
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:26,000
and light it during close combat. The result was a pole arm that spewed flame and burning debris directly at anyone within arms reach, turning an ordinary

26
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:35,000
spear into something considerably more terrifying to face in a melee. Defenders used fire lances during the siege of Deon in 1132 against attacking forces

27
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:43,000
climbing their walls. And by every account, the weapon worked exactly as intended, scorching and disorienting attackers in a way conventional spears

28
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:51,000
simply couldn't match. The psychological effect alone was significant. Soldiers who had spent their entire lives training to fight with blades and spears

29
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:59,000
suddenly found themselves facing an enemy who could project fire itself. And that kind of shock often mattered as much on a medieval battlefield as the

30
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:07,000
actual physical damage inflicted. Once gunpowder had proven itself useful in actual combat, the pace of innovation accelerated quickly, and each successive

31
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:16,000
improvement followed a fairly logical, almost obvious next step from whatever came before it. Makers of fire lances began stuffing pellets and shards of

32
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:23,000
pottery into the bamboo tubes alongside the gunpowder, transforming a weapon that simply produced flame into one that

33
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:31,000
also flung small projectiles outward with real force. This was a genuinely significant conceptual leap because it shifted the weapon's purpose from pure

34
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:40,000
intimidation and burning toward actual physical penetrating injury delivered at a distance. The foundational concept underlying every firearm that would

35
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:48,000
eventually follow. The next major limitation was the bamboo itself, which simply couldn't handle the pressure that more powerful gunpowder charges

36
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:57,000
generated without splitting or bursting apart in the user's own hands, occasionally injuring the very soldier wielding it. Once Chinese craftsmen

37
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:05,000
learned to cast bronze tubes strong enough to contain that pressure safely, the hand cannon was born. A weapon that used the expanding gas from gunpowder

38
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:13,000
combustion to launch a single solid projectile with considerably more force and considerably more range than any bamboo fire lance ever could. By the

39
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:21,000
13th century, metal-barreled hand cannons were already in regular use across China, representing a genuine technological leap in both reliability

40
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:29,000
and effectiveness compared to the earlier bamboo based weapons. Chinese engineers also scaled the same basic concept upward, producing devices with

41
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:38,000
names like the Thunderclap bomb and the heavenshaking cannon, weapons capable of delivering genuinely high impact explosions on a battlefield and reducing

42
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:45,000
fortified positions that had previously seemed unassalable. When the Mongols invaded China in the 1200s, they encountered these gunpowder weapons

43
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:52,000
firsthand during sieges like the famous defense of Kiong in 1232, and the experience left a lasting impression.

44
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Rather than simply being defeated by the new technology, the Mongols absorbed it.

45
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:04,000
And once they had conquered China and established the UN dynasty, they carried gunpowder weaponry with them as they pushed westward into new conquests

46
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:12,000
across Asia and toward the Islamic world. This is genuinely one of history's more important and underappreciated technology transfers

47
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:20,000
because Mongol military campaigns functioned almost like an accidental delivery system, carrying gunpowder knowledge across enormous distances far

48
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:28,000
faster than trade caravans or diplomatic exchanges ever could have managed on their own, spreading a Chinese invention across the breadth of Eurasia within the

49
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:37,000
span of just a few generations. It's also worth noting that gunpowder weapons didn't develop along a single straight line during this period. Alongside hand

50
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:45,000
cannons and fire lances, Chinese military engineers also developed rockets and fire arrows, gunpowder propelled projectiles that found use not

51
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:52,000
just in China, but eventually in Korea and India as well, traveling along separate but overlapping routes from the

52
00:06:52,000 --> 00:07:01,000
hand cannon's own path westward. These rocket weapons never fully disappeared the way the fire lance eventually did once true guns matured. Instead, they

53
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:10,000
persisted as a distinct branch of gunpowder technology, one that would resurface centuries later in European warfare. And much later still, in the

54
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:19,000
earliest ancestors of modern military and space rocketry, the story of gunpowder, in other words, doesn't just lead to the gun. It branches with one

55
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:28,000
path heading toward the firearm and an entirely separate path heading toward the rocket. Both rooted in the same accidental discovery made by alchemists

56
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:36,000
who wanted nothing to do with either outcome. From there, gunpowder technology spread outward along multiple overlapping routes simultaneously,

57
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:44,000
reaching the Islamic world and eventually Europe through a combination of military contact, trade, and the broader wave of cross-cultural exchange

58
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:53,000
that characterized the 13th and 14th centuries. Historians studying this period point to a broader pattern worth appreciating. Gunpowder wasn't the only

59
00:07:53,000 --> 00:08:04,000
major Chinese invention making its way westward during this era. paper, printing, and the magnetic compass all traveled the same general routes during roughly the same stretch of centuries.

60
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:11,000
Part of a much larger flow of technology and knowledge, moving from east to west, long before anyone had coined the term globalization.

61
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:19,000
Some historians have noted that early European cannons bear such a close structural resemblance to Chinese designs that a direct transfer of

62
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:27,000
knowledge seems considerably more plausible than independent invention happening to arrive at nearly identical solutions by coincidence. Once the

63
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:35,000
technology reached Europe in the late 13th century, it landed in a political and military landscape that gunpowder weapons would over the following

64
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:43,000
centuries completely and permanently transform, eventually dismantling systems of feudal military power that had stood for hundreds of years. The

65
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:52,000
transformation wasn't instant. Early European hand cannons, much like their Chinese predecessors, were crude, slow to reload, and difficult to aim with any

66
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:59,000
real precision. essentially a small cannon mounted awkwardly on the end of a pole. You loaded powder and projectile

67
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:08,000
into the open end, touched a smoldering ember or a length of slow burning cord to a small touch hole, and hoped with considerably more optimism than

68
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:16,000
confidence that the resulting explosion would actually send the projectile somewhere useful. But even in this primitive form, the weapon carried a

69
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:24,000
psychological and practical impact that traditional melee weapons simply couldn't match. Castle walls that had withtood traditional siege equipment for

70
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:31,000
generations began falling to cannon fire. Mounted knights in heavy armor, the dominant military force across

71
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:39,000
medieval Europe for centuries, found themselves increasingly vulnerable to a weapon that didn't care how much steel you were wearing since enough

72
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:47,000
gunpowder-driven force could punch straight through it regardless. The entire social and military order built around expensive, heavily armored

73
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:54,000
cavalry began to wobble because a relatively cheap weapon in the hands of a far less expensively trained soldier

74
00:09:54,000 --> 00:10:03,000
could now neutralize an opponent who had spent a lifetime and a fortune preparing for combat in the traditional way. This shift had consequences far beyond the

75
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:12,000
battlefield itself, reshaping the very architecture of European warfare. As cannons grew steadily more powerful and increasingly effective against vertical

76
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:20,000
stone walls, traditional castle design built around tall, thin walls meant to repel scaling attackers and arrows

77
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:29,000
became dangerously obsolete almost overnight in historical terms. Military engineers responded by developing the Star Fort and the Bastion. lower,

78
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:37,000
angled, thickly reinforced fortifications specifically designed to deflect and absorb cannon fire rather than simply standing tall and hoping for

79
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:45,000
the best. The medieval castle, an architectural form that had dominated European landscapes for centuries, was gradually replaced by structures that

80
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:53,000
looked almost nothing like it. All because of a weapon that had originated half a world away in the hands of monks chasing immortality. The next genuinely

81
00:10:53,000 --> 00:11:01,000
important leap came in the late 15th century with the invention of the matchlock mechanism. And this single development is what finally turned

82
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:08,000
gunpowder weapons from clumsy novelties into the backbone of organized infantry warfare across the continent. The

83
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:16,000
fundamental problem with earlier hand cannons was that actually firing one required two hands and considerable coordination. one hand to steady the

84
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:24,000
weapon, the other to apply a burning ember or cord directly to the touch hole at exactly the right moment, all while

85
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:32,000
trying to actually aim at a moving target on a chaotic battlefield. The matchlock mechanism solved this elegantly by attaching a slow burning

86
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:40,000
cord held in a curved clamp called a serpentine directly to the weapon itself. Pulling the trigger lowered that already burning core directly into a

87
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:48,000
small pan of priming powder, igniting the main charge automatically and freeing the shooter to actually hold and aim the weapon with both hands right up

88
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:56,000
until the moment of firing. This may sound like a modest mechanical tweak, but it was genuinely transformative because it was the first mechanical

89
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:03,000
firing device ever applied to a small arm. And it allowed for the first time in history a single trained soldier to

90
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:11,000
fire a gunpowder weapon with some real degree of aimed precision rather than simply pointing roughly in the right direction and hoping for the best. By

91
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:19,000
the early 15th century, matchlock arabuses were appearing in growing numbers across Western Europe, particularly in Spain, France, and the

92
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:26,000
territories of the Holy Roman Empire, and the weapons spread rapidly from there across the entire breadth of Eurasia. The Ottoman Empire already

93
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:35,000
experienced with gunpowder weaponry since the 14th century incorporated matchlocks directly into the elite Janiseri Corps that formed the backbone

94
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:43,000
of Ottoman military power using the weapon to great effect in engagements such as the Battle of Goa in 1510 alongside the Portuguese forces that

95
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:52,000
also relied heavily on matchlock weaponry during that same period. In 1543, Portuguese traders introduced matchlock technology to Japan, where

96
00:12:52,000 --> 00:13:00,000
local gunsmiths refined the design so thoroughly that Japanese matchlocks known as tanagashima came to be regarded as some of the finest crafted firearms

97
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:08,000
anywhere in the world during that period. Prised for their precision and craftsmanship even by European observers. China itself, having started

98
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:16,000
this entire chain of innovation centuries earlier, ended up reabsorbing an improved version of its own original invention, developing matchlock weapons

99
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:24,000
known as birding guns, specifically to counter ongoing Mongol incursions along its northern frontier, a genuinely strange and fitting historical loop for

100
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:33,000
a technology that began as a failed immortality potion in that same general region. Matchlock musketss eventually became heavy, powerful enough to need a

101
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:42,000
forked rest just to support the barrel while aiming, weighing as much as 25 pounds in some configurations, but capable of punching through plate armor

102
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:50,000
at ranges that made traditional swords and pikes increasingly irrelevant on an open battlefield. Because reloading and firing a matchlock took considerable

103
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:59,000
time and because the weapon left the user briefly vulnerable during that process, musketeers typically required protection from melee attacks, usually

104
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:07,000
provided by pikemen standing alongside them. This combination of pikes and shot came to dominate European battlefields from the 16th into the early 17th

105
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:15,000
century. an elaborate, carefully choreographed tactical system requiring extensive training to coordinate effectively with musketeers firing from

106
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:22,000
behind or between rows of pikemen in tightly organized formations. Outside Europe, many armies that had adopted

107
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:31,000
matchlocks retained their traditional formations rather than developing this same dense, disciplined infantry coordination, lacking either the

108
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:39,000
resources or the specific tactical incentives that drove European armies toward this particular evolution in organized combined arms warfare. The

109
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:47,000
matchlock had one obvious persistent weakness that everyone using it understood immediately. It required an

110
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:55,000
open, slow burning flame at all times, which made it nearly useless in wet weather, gave away a soldier's position in darkness, and required constant

111
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:03,000
management of burning cord, even when nothing was happening on the battlefield at all. The solution arrived gradually through several intermediate steps worth

112
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:12,000
understanding. The wheel lock, developed in the early 16th century, used a spring-loaded wheel spinning rapidly against a piece of pyite to generate

113
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:19,000
sparks, eliminating the need for a constantly burning match entirely. This made wheel locks considerably more

114
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:27,000
practical for cavalry who needed a weapon they could carry loaded and ready without tending an open flame while on horseback. and German mercenaries along

115
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:36,000
with English cavalry units became particularly associated with this mechanism during the 16th and early 17th centuries. But wheel locks were

116
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:46,000
expensive and mechanically complex to manufacture which kept them from ever fully replacing the cheaper, simpler matchlock among ordinary infantry who

117
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:53,000
continued carrying the older, more affordable design well into the following century. Even as wealthier cavalry men rode with the more advanced

118
00:15:53,000 --> 00:16:00,000
alternative, the true breakthrough came with the flint lock. Generally credited to French gunsmith Moran Lab Bourgeois,

119
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:08,000
who developed the mechanism in its recognizable modern form around 1610 to 1615. The flint lock used a

120
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:17,000
spring-loaded hammer holding a piece of flint, which struck a steel surface called a frisen to generate sparks directly into the priming pan, igniting

121
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:26,000
the charge. It was faster to operate than a matchlock, dramatically more reliable in poor weather, and required no open flame to maintain at all, which

122
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:34,000
also meant soldiers no longer gave away their position in the dark simply by carrying a lit weapon. By the 1630s, the flint lock mechanism had spread rapidly

123
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:40,000
across Europe, with the Dutch States Army among the first to formally adopt it as a standardissue infantry weapon.

124
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:49,000
And within a few decades, it had become the dominant firearm mechanism across essentially every major European military, a position it would hold for

125
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:56,000
roughly two centuries, right through the colonial expansion, the Napoleonic Wars, and the American Revolution that

126
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:03,000
followed, where flint lock musketss carried by both colonial militias and British regulars defined the entire

127
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:10,000
conflict from start to finish. The flint lock's arrival coincided with another genuinely important innovation, the

128
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:19,000
socket bayonet introduced in the late 17th century, which attached around the outside of the barrel via a metal ring rather than plugging directly into the

129
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:27,000
muzzle the way earlier plug bayonet designs had, which rendered the gun unable to fire while the bayonet was actually attached. This seemingly small

130
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:36,000
change meant a soldier could fix a bayonet to his musket and still fire it normally. Combining ranged firepower and close combat capability in a single

131
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:44,000
weapon for the first time. The practical effect was enormous. Armies no longer needed dedicated pikemen standing alongside their musketeers for

132
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:54,000
protection since every soldier carrying a flint lock and bayonet could now function as both shooter and spearman depending on what the moment required.

133
00:17:54,000 --> 00:18:00,000
This single combined development, sometimes called the infantry revolution, simplified training,

134
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:07,000
simplified logistics, and fundamentally reshaped how European armies organized themselves on the battlefield, allowing

135
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:16,000
regiments to become more versatile and capable of engaging enemies at range or in close combat without needing to reorganize entirely between the two

136
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:23,000
modes of fighting. The bayonet charge also carried its own distinct psychological weight on the battlefield since the sight of an advancing line of

137
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:31,000
soldiers with fixed bayonets often broke enemy morale before a single shot or thrust ever actually connected. The

138
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:38,000
flint lock's roughly two century reign finally ended in the early 19th century with the arrival of the percussion cap

139
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:46,000
system, a small metal cap containing a shock sensitive chemical compound that ignited instantly when struck by a hammer, eliminating the need for loose

140
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:54,000
priming powder, flint, and steel entirely. This final ignition innovation rendered every previous mechanism,

141
00:18:54,000 --> 00:19:01,000
matchlock, wheel lock, and flint lock alike, essentially obsolete within a few decades. and it marked the last major

142
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:10,000
milestone in the centuries long chain of refinements that had begun with monks chasing immortality in 9th century China. From there, the pace of change

143
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:18,000
only accelerated, leading toward repeating rifles, rapid advances in metallurgy and manufacturing, and eventually weapons that would have been

144
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:26,000
utterly unrecognizable to the soldiers who once stood in disciplined lines with pikes and matchlocks waiting for their turn to fire. Looking back across this

145
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:33,000
entire arc, from a towist alchemy lab in 9th century China to flint lock musketss dominating European battlefields nearly

146
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:40,000
8 centuries later, the story of how humans invented guns is really a story about accident, accumulation, and

147
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:48,000
migration happening together over an enormous span of time. Nobody set out on day one to invent the firearm. A group

148
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:55,000
of monks looking for immortality stumbled onto an explosive chemical reaction and very nearly burned their own beards off in the process. Military

149
00:19:55,000 --> 00:20:04,000
engineers watching that explosive reaction gradually realized its violence could be channeled. First through a tube strapped to a spear, then through a cast

150
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:12,000
bronze cylinder, then through increasingly sophisticated mechanical systems designed to ignite that same basic chemical reaction more reliably,

151
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:21,000
more quickly, and with less risk to the person holding the weapon. Each individual improvement, the shift from bamboo to bronze, from open flame to

152
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:28,000
spring-loaded mechanism, from matchlock to flint lock, addressed a specific practical problem that soldiers and engineers had actually encountered using

153
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:37,000
the version that came before it. None of it required some singular genius moment of total invention. It required centuries of incremental refinement

154
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:44,000
carried across continents by trade routes, military campaigns, and the kind of slow cultural exchange that quietly

155
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:53,000
reshapes the world while nobody's watching closely enough to notice it happening in real time. Passing hands between Chinese engineers, Mongol

156
00:20:53,000 --> 00:21:01,000
conquerors, Ottoman janiseries, Japanese gunsmiths, and European craftsmen. Each group adding their own refinements along

157
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:08,000
the way. The gun that would eventually end the dominance of mounted knights, reshape castle architecture, and ultimately determine the outcome of

158
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:11,000
colonial conquests and continental wars across multiple centuries began its existence as a mistake. made by people who were never trying to build a weapon at all.
